    "case_id": "565",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old man presented with a 3-month history of polyarthralgia over the bilateral wrists, knees, ankles, and left shoulder, associated with early morning stiffness and swelling of the affected joints. Once-daily celecoxib provided significant but incomplete pain relief. He noted diffuse swelling over both feet and ankles and mild puffiness of his left hand. He had no rashes, nail changes, uveitis, or constitutional symptoms. He had a 6-month duration of cough associated with occasional blood streaks, with no previous smoking history.\n\nSynovitis was elicited over both ankles. Bilateral lower limb pitting edema up to the ankles was noted. Reduced breath sounds were heard over the right upper zone. There was no clubbing or cervical lymphadenopathy.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  - Erythrocyte sedimentation rate: 40.0 mm/h\n  - C-reactive protein: 41.0 mg/L\n  - Rheumatoid factor: negative\n  - Serum creatinine: 73.0 µmol/L (0.83 mg/dL)\n  - Serum albumin: 37.0 g/L (within normal range)\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  - Radiographs of hands, knees, ankles, and left shoulder: unremarkable for erosions\n  - Chest radiograph: 8.1×6.4-cm mass in the right upper zone\n  - Computed tomography of chest: lesion with paratracheal lymphadenopathy\n    Image Title: Figure 1\n    Image Description: Computed tomography scan of the chest demonstrating a right upper zone mass with paratracheal lymphadenopathy\n\n- Pathology:\n  - Endobronchial ultrasound-guided fine-needle aspiration biopsy of mediastinal lymph nodes: ALK-positive lung adenocarcinoma with mucinous features\n\n- Additional Studies:\n  - Positron emission tomography-computed tomography of full body: small foci suspicious for metastatic disease",
